00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.950 align:start position:0%
 
i'm joined by the world health  organization special envoy for the  global covid 19 response  and chair of global health at imperial  college dr david nabarro dr nabarro  thank you very much  for being with us so the government now  considering  uh quarantine hotels mandatory  quarantine  for arrivals in to the uk  is that the right response  certainly the need to isolate  people who may be at risk of bringing  covid into communities is  recognized as important especially  if countries are concerned to try to  reduce the risk  of new cases coming in and particularly  if they are  of a new variant but i do want to stress  that isolation is a key part of dealing  with kovid  wherever we are because the only way you  can interrupt transmission  is by isolating people with the disease  until they're free of it  either you treat them or if they don't  need treatment they must stay at home  this is key and without isolation being  done  effectively actually getting on top of  the pandemic is really difficult  by effective isolation then what would  you like  to see what does that mean  the countries that have used isolation  effectively  as as was presented in in the  introduction just now  uh countries who are requiring  people to actually stay away from other  people  and to keep away from other people if  people  do not isolate then there is a huge risk  of the virus spreading so isolation must  be  complete and one of the findings of  countries that have been successful  is that isolation is best done if it's  done under supervision  that's why the plan to introduce  quarantine hotels  makes a lot of sense it's certainly  what's worked in other parts of the  world  and it's certainly more effective than  asking people to self-isolate  unfortunately they don't necessarily for  a mix of reasons find it easy to do that  so are you suggesting that it should be  not just for arrivals into the country  but that anyone who tests positive  should go to a facility to isolate  what we're saying is that if isolation  is going to be done  it should be done properly and  effectively  whatever means a government or other  authority can use  to ensure isolation is effective should  be used  i think in practice a government has to  decide which is the technique that's  most useful  and certainly several countries have  moved towards using  hotels or some form of supervised  quarantine  to try to actually make sure that people  do isolate  and are supported while they isolate  with food and with  medicines if they need them another  requirements  the government has always avoided this  despite other countries as  we quoted australia and new zealand  doing it but now the concern  from new variants 77 cases of the south  africa variant  now in the uk nine of one of the uh  brazilian  variants questions about the vaccine  efficacy we've had some information from  moderner today at mixed news i would say  reading it what is your analysis of what  they've had to say  well i i've read the reports from  moderna i've got to read them in more  detail  i think the general view that we have is  that  new variants are going to go on emerging  all the time and the more virus there's  around in the world  the more likely the hood there is of new  variants  coming uh into notice and so  i would say that it's really important  to continue  surveillance for the new variants and  then to do the necessary laboratory  work to see whether or not the vaccines  work against them  the modern information is promising  as is the information from other vaccine  producers  most of them seem to feel that of the  variants that have  appeared so far the vaccines appear to  work  the question is will vaccines go on  working  against all newly emerging variants  the answer is we can't say at this stage  and that's why we are encouraging  all governments that can to continue  looking at the coronavirus cases that  are appearing in their communities  and check to see whether they are  showing any signs of being  one of the existing variants or even a  completely new variant  we haven't seen before this will be a  continuing reality  of this pandemic because all viruses  mutate  and it requirement is to look and see  whether or not mutations appear  that change either the transmissibility  of the virus  or the impact of the virus on people or  the degree to which  vaccines protect against the variants as  they emerge  yeah and so what we're seeing so far at  least in the lab  analysis is the moderna vaccine  effective against the variant  that was identified in the uk six times  less effective against the  south africa variant so so what's the  answer that potentially looking at a  third  booster dose well i  i mean the usual way that we act when  a virus mutates is that we look at the  vaccine and see whether the vaccine  needs to be changed  so that it is active against the variant  this is a process that's possible it  takes a bit of time because the  vaccine manufacturer has to refashion  the vaccine and then test it out  but it's doable i think the only issue  that we face is that it will take  a significant number of weeks perhaps  even months  for a vaccine to be adapted to a new  variant  and in that interval the variant can  cause a  considerable challenge for societies  what it means is  we've got to have very close cooperation  between governments checking to see  how the variants are emerging and then  the vaccine manufacturers to check that  their vaccines work  and then once finally once we've got the  vaccine adjusted  then vaccination schedules have to be  adapted what i'm suggesting is  it's a complex process and that's why  the world health organization is  encouraging all governments  to work with the who on their  immunization strategies  it's going to require constant  adaptation  in the light of new information and  really no country  i think can reasonably do this alone  we've got to have  all countries working together to be  expect to make sure that we're ahead of  the virus  and anticipating and then reacting to  variants  when they emerge it's best if countries  do this  as a whole and approach the problem as a  global problem  rather than operating independently with  their own  separate regimes and in terms of  independent regimes  the uk government has decided on a  12-week gap between  uh first dose and second dose of the  vaccine the world health organization  has expressed some concern about that  the jcvi have defended that and  suggested that perhaps it might even  lead to greater  immunity i want to get your thoughts on  that  this is a difficult one you see on the  one hand  the manufacturers have tested their  vaccines  using a particular schedule for rolling  out  and on the other hand governments are  trying as hard as they can to get as  many people as possible protected  that's led some governments to extend  the interval between vaccine doses  yet the manufacturers testing was not  done using long intervals inevitably  the world health organization which has  to work with the data provided by the  manufacturers  will come down in favor of using  the vaccine as was prescribed by the  manufacturers and not going for  alternatives  the last thing any of us wants to do is  to question a government strategy so  instead what we're doing  is encouraging all governments that are  using longer dose intervals as part of  their strategy  to be very clear with the public and  with professionals  about the rationale for the extended  doses in the end it's a government's  decision what it does  the world health organization can offer  recommendations  but we can't actually have an  alternative approach to national  governments  this is a very challenging time each  government  is really trying to get as many people  protected as possible  but we we do have to keep stating that  the manufacturers of the vaccines  offered a particular regime when they  presented they  date their data to regulators and we  need to stick with those  those uh pieces of information that come  through from the manufacturers  when when we offer our recommendations  to the world

